1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,257
Vanakkam. This is Liam, in for Bear, with chapter three of INFO 7375, Computational

2
00:00:04,277 --> 00:00:08,535
Skepticism for AI, at Northeastern. This film is about blank cells. The reason a

3
00:00:08,555 --> 00:00:13,423
value is missing decides whether filling it in is safe. Sort the rows by what you

4
00:00:13,443 --> 00:00:18,311
can see, and some blanks line up. Some never will. The trap: the worst kind of

5
00:00:18,331 --> 00:00:20,450
missing looks exactly like the harmless kind.

6
00:00:20,520 --> 00:00:24,560
Why should you care? Every dataset you inherit has blanks, and the first fix anyone

7
00:00:24,580 --> 00:00:28,619
reaches for is the average. When the reason is harmless, the average lands on the

8
00:00:28,639 --> 00:00:32,950
truth. When the people who left it blank are different, it drifts in a direction you

9
00:00:32,970 --> 00:00:36,739
cannot measure. Knowing the three kinds tells you which question no plot can answer

10
00:00:36,759 --> 00:00:37,280
for you.

11
00:00:37,350 --> 00:00:42,547
The real question: before I fill them in, should I ask why it is missing? How much

12
00:00:42,567 --> 00:00:46,536
is missing is identical here: eighteen percent, both times. The count cannot tell

13
00:00:46,556 --> 00:00:48,070
them apart. The reason can.

14
00:00:48,140 --> 00:00:52,438
Three words you'll hear. A missingness matrix: every row a thin line, every column a

15
00:00:52,458 --> 00:00:57,044
stripe, dark where a value is absent. Impute: fill a blank with an estimate, such as

16
00:00:57,064 --> 00:01:01,938
the average of the values you can see. And a subgroup: rows that share a trait, such

17
00:01:01,958 --> 00:01:03,090
as everyone under twenty-five.

18
00:01:03,160 --> 00:01:07,367
Two tables of the same fifty people. In each, the income column is eighteen percent

19
00:01:07,387 --> 00:01:11,594
blank: nine rows out of fifty. Look at the dark cells. Scattered, in both. Same

20
00:01:11,614 --> 00:01:16,102
count, same look. Fill both with the average income you can see. One fill lands on

21
00:01:16,122 --> 00:01:18,920
the truth. The other lands somewhere else. Which is which?

22
00:01:18,990 --> 00:01:22,834
Start with the easy case: a third table, where nine income cells were wiped at

23
00:01:22,854 --> 00:01:26,956
random, a coin flip, nothing to do with the person. Fill the nine with the average

24
00:01:26,976 --> 00:01:31,077
of the forty-one you can see. We built this table, so we can reveal the true

25
00:01:31,097 --> 00:01:35,456
average. The fill lands right on it. Wipe a different nine at random: it lands on it

26
00:01:35,476 --> 00:01:38,290
again. Random loss shrinks your sample. It does not tilt it.

27
00:01:38,360 --> 00:01:42,957
Now table A. Same fill, same average of the visible rows. This time the dot lands to

28
00:01:42,977 --> 00:01:47,302
the right of the truth. Too high. Something about who is blank pulled it. Look along

29
00:01:47,322 --> 00:01:51,103
the age stripe, beside each dark cell: every blank row belongs to someone under

30
00:01:51,123 --> 00:01:55,177
twenty-five. In this table the young earn less, and they are the ones who skipped

31
00:01:55,197 --> 00:01:55,720
the question.

32
00:01:55,790 --> 00:02:00,363
The natural fix: fill each blank with the average of its own age group. Do it for

33
00:02:00,383 --> 00:02:04,956
table A, and the dot slides back onto the truth. Now do exactly the same thing for

34
00:02:04,976 --> 00:02:09,009
table B, whose blanks look just as scattered. The dot barely moves. It stays low.

35
00:02:09,029 --> 00:02:11,170
And nothing in the table tells you why.

36
00:02:11,240 --> 00:02:15,852
Here's the move. Stop reading the matrix in file order. Sort the rows by a column

37
00:02:15,872 --> 00:02:21,063
you can see, and watch where the blanks go. Sort table A by age: the nine dark cells

38
00:02:21,083 --> 00:02:24,537
slide together, all inside the under-twenty-fives. Sort table B by age: still

39
00:02:24,557 --> 00:02:28,590
scattered. By city: scattered. A block means the blanks depend on something you can

40
00:02:28,610 --> 00:02:28,880
see.

41
00:02:28,950 --> 00:02:33,667
Table B stays scattered under every column it has. So here is the one column it

42
00:02:33,687 --> 00:02:37,517
doesn't have: each person's true income, the value that was never written down.

43
00:02:37,537 --> 00:02:42,254
Before I sort by it, commit. Where will table B's nine blanks go? And the random

44
00:02:42,274 --> 00:02:42,550
table's?

45
00:02:42,620 --> 00:02:47,726
Sort. Table B's blanks slide into one block at the very top: the nine highest

46
00:02:47,746 --> 00:02:52,168
earners. The random table's stay scattered. From inside the data, those two looked

47
00:02:52,188 --> 00:02:52,510
identical.

48
00:02:52,580 --> 00:02:56,364
These deserve names, and they come from Donald Rubin's work on missing data. Missing

49
00:02:56,384 --> 00:03:00,167
completely at random: the chance of a blank depends on nothing. Missing at random:

50
00:03:00,187 --> 00:03:04,514
it depends on something you can see, like age. Missing not at random: it depends on

51
00:03:04,534 --> 00:03:09,133
the missing value itself. The first two, a sort can show you. The third, no sort you

52
00:03:09,153 --> 00:03:10,220
can run ever will.

53
00:03:10,290 --> 00:03:13,678
Same shape, different table. A study's final measurement, blank for the patients who

54
00:03:13,698 --> 00:03:17,348
left before the end. Sort by anything recorded: scattered. The sort that would line

55
00:03:17,368 --> 00:03:21,804
them up is who got sicker, which is the very thing that's missing. So you don't find

56
00:03:21,824 --> 00:03:26,260
this kind with a plot. You find it by asking: who would not report this, and why?

57
00:03:26,330 --> 00:03:30,131
Back to the two tables. Both eighteen percent blank, both scattered in file order.

58
00:03:30,151 --> 00:03:35,045
Sort by age: table A lines up, so fill within the age group and you land on the

59
00:03:35,065 --> 00:03:39,139
truth. Table B never lines up, and its average sits low, biased in exactly the

60
00:03:39,159 --> 00:03:42,687
direction you cannot measure. Not taught here: how to choose an imputation method,

61
00:03:42,707 --> 00:03:45,690
or rows a join dropped before any matrix could see them.

62
00:03:45,760 --> 00:03:49,488
Let's recap with Claude. Missing is not one thing. If the blanks depend on nothing,

63
00:03:49,508 --> 00:03:53,486
an average fill lands on the truth. If they depend on something you can see, like

64
00:03:53,506 --> 00:03:57,733
age, sort by it, fill within the group, and you recover it. If they depend on the

65
00:03:57,753 --> 00:04:01,481
missing value itself, every fill drifts, and no plot can show you. For that kind,

66
00:04:01,501 --> 00:04:03,230
ask who would not report, and why.

67
00:04:03,300 --> 00:04:07,521
Your turn. Paste this into Claude: A clinic dataset has a column pain_score that is

68
00:04:07,541 --> 00:04:10,913
18% blank, and the missingness matrix shows the blanks cluster in evening

69
00:04:10,933 --> 00:04:14,871
appointments. Before writing any code, predict: MCAR, MAR, or MNAR? Then give me one

70
00:04:14,891 --> 00:04:19,394
story under which it really is MAR, and one under which it is actually MNAR. For

71
00:04:19,414 --> 00:04:23,917
each story, say which way an average fill would move the mean pain score, and name

72
00:04:23,937 --> 00:04:28,441
one question I should ask the clinic that no plot of this data could answer. Two

73
00:04:28,461 --> 00:04:32,964
things to look for. Did it commit to a prediction before it reached for code? And

74
00:04:32,984 --> 00:04:37,205
does its MNAR story name who would not report, and why, instead of recommending a

75
00:04:37,225 --> 00:04:37,770
fancier fill?

76
00:04:37,840 --> 00:04:41,885
Missing Is Not One Thing. At Nik Bear Brown.
